00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
This is LAist 89.3. I'm Nereida Moreno.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Extreme heat is becoming more common and more deadly across the globe.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
A new analysis from Reuters combed through extreme heat data on Earth for the past six decades,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
and they coined a new term, heat storms, to describe that phenomenon.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Reuters editor Ben Welsh has been putting together the data on heat storms, and he joins us now to discuss. Ben, welcome.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Thank you for having me. You know, I'm a loyal listener to LAist and a sustaining member.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
I'm honored to be here and to speak to your great audience.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.000
We really appreciate you for being here. So first, what are heat storms and how did you come up with this metric?

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:01.000
This all started a while ago when my colleague and I, Maurice Tamman, set out to answer what we thought would be a pretty simple question.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.000
We asked ourselves, are heat waves more common than they used to be? It seems to feel like it.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
And since we're data journalists, what we did is we went off looking for some science and data we could use to answer the question.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.000
And that's when we learned something that surprised me and Mo. There is no official scientific definition of a heat wave.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
What counted as extreme heat was culturally determined. Different places set different standards.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.000
And while climate scientists spent a lot of energy promoting things like the average global temperature,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
it turns out they've never actually settled on one way to measure what counts as a heat wave.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
So we decided to try to do it ourselves. We interviewed some of the world's leading experts.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
We reviewed how other metrics are established and basically did a bunch of homework.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
And then we settled on a measuring stick that we think is simple and grounded in science.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Yeah, tell us more about that measuring stick.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Sure. So we start by taking a 30-year sample of what used to be normal for every point on the planet.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
The best data available today lets you go back to 1960 to 1990.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
We then compare today's temperatures to that period.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
And when the high ranks in the top 10% of what used to be normal for five or more days in a row, we call that a heat wave.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
We then applied that measuring stick to every day since 1960 and put the results on the map.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
That's when things really got interesting.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Back in the '60s and '70s, heat waves, under our definition, were rare, isolated events.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:38.000
But in recent years, as Earth has gotten warmer, they've grown into these massive cascading blobs that blanket huge sections of the planet for months or even years at a time.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
And looking at those maps, Mo and I realized we just didn't have the words to properly describe what we were seeing.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Heat wave just didn't feel like enough.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:57.000
So we coined the term heat storm. And to qualify as a heat storm under our system, a heat wave has to cover at least 1% of Earth, which is about half the size of Canada.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Right. Like you said, it's our new normal.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
And Ben, your analysis also found that the largest heat storm on record happened between 2023 and 2024.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.000
So how did that affect different parts of the Earth?

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Yeah, last week on Reuters.com, we published the story of the storm, which we call heat storm Vera, which lasted for more than a year and reached almost 85% of Earth's surface as it morphed over time.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Our reporting team chronicled its effects on people thousands of miles apart.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Football players in Texas were swooning under the Friday night lights.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Philippine students roasted in un-air-conditioned classrooms and pink dolphins in the Amazon rainforest even turned belly up, effectively cooked alive in the Jacuzzi-like waters there.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.000
And how did that heat storm affect us here in Southern California?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.000
SoCal was part of Vera's story from the very start.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:56.000
We named the storm on July 29, 2023, when an older heat storm split into smaller pieces.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.000
At that time, Vera stretched from the Central Pacific across California and all the way to the coast of Spain.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.000
On its first day, Vera drove up the heat in Palm Springs to 118 degrees.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.000
That's the hottest day of an already hot month and well above what used to be normal, even out there in the desert.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Elsewhere in the region, air quality officials were warning that heat was increasing smog in the air.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.000
And that night, the lows in Palm Springs didn't get below 90 degrees.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
So you started naming these heat storms and you even launched an online heat storm tracker.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Ben, why is it important to name them?

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:39.000
And do you anticipate that, you know, public agencies might consider adopting this naming framework in the future?

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.000
I mean, I get it if people out there in listener land are rolling their eyes at yet another weather word, right?

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.000
But the truth is we did not set out to do this.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.000
We invented this term because we felt that, quote, heat wave simply didn't do justice to what we were seeing in the data.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.000
As climate change is strengthening its grip, something bigger is happening to our planet.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.000
We don't claim this term is perfect.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.000
We don't expect everyone to instantly adopt it.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.000
But if you can't name something, how can you talk about it at all?

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.000
We've been speaking with Reuters editor Ben Welsh. Ben, thank you so much for joining us.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Thank you for having me.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Stay cool, L.A.
